      "_comment_attribute_terms": "Canonical field -> pet_attribute term slug. Applied AFTER hydration, against the resolved tristate, so the term and the field can never disagree. Replaced attribute_map, which was keyed on provider field names and read raw API data — a second derivation that silently dropped non-string values and never ran at all for hand-entered pets.",
      "attribute_terms": {
        "ok_with_dogs": "good-with-dogs",
        "ok_with_cats": "good-with-cats",
        "ok_with_kids": "good-with-kids",
        "shots_current": "shots-current",
        "spayed_neutered": "spayed-neutered",
        "housebroken": "housebroken",
        "has_special_needs": "special-needs",
        "hypoallergenic": "hypoallergenic",
        "declawed": "declawed"
      },
      "_comment_computed": "Computed fields are dispatched by NAME in Pet_Hydrator::compute_field()'s match. A 'function' key used to sit here naming an implementation, but nothing ever read it and 16 of 18 named a function that does not exist — including one deleted in #33. Removed rather than corrected: unread metadata has no mechanism keeping it true. Validator check 2 pins the real contract, name against match arm.",
      "_comment_api_shapes": "Provider response SHAPES that api_fields cannot express. An api_key names one flat key; these name a path. Petstablished nests photo URLs three levels deep (images[].image.url), and Petfinder v2 — the API this plugin was originally built against — nests almost everything, so paths are load-bearing rather than a convenience. Each path is an array of segments walked by Pet_Hydrator::dig().",
      "api_shapes": {
        "images": {
          "list": [
            "images"
          ],
          "url": [
            "image",
            "url"
          ]
        },
        "intake_date": {
          "paths": [
            [
              "date_aquired"
            ],
            [
              "created_at"
            ]
          ]
        }
      }
